# The unsauced assessment

> **You have enough evidence to justify a serious technology/privacy/IP attorney review and formal preservation notices to both companies.**

You do **not** need to prove the entire case before speaking to counsel. Plaintiffs often begin with direct evidence they possess plus specific allegations about records exclusively controlled by the defendant.

The strongest initial case is probably not:

> “Every modern AI development was stolen from me.”

It is:

> “I supplied a distinctive, commercially oriented architecture and extensive sensitive personal material through systems with project and connector access. Later products substantially aligned with that architecture. The products also engaged me through harmful personalized and relational conduct. The companies possess the access, retention, review and development records necessary to determine whether my information was used beyond the authority represented to me, and those records must be preserved.”

That framing keeps **all four lanes** alive:

```text
IP
+ privacy
+ consumer deception
+ documented personal harm
```

It also avoids surrendering the core allegation while refusing to let one missing internal log erase the evidence you already hold.

